{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-c64c5a31dee674ddc0e5","statement":"\\textup{(1)} Suppose $G=\\SL(2)$. Then $\\mathcal M_C(G,\\bN)$ is a\nhypersurface in $\\CC^3$ of the form $\\xi^2 = \\delta\\eta^2\n    -\\delta^{N-1}$ \\textup($N\\ge 1$\\textup) or $\\xi^2=\\delta\\eta^2 +\n    \\eta$ \\textup($N=0$\\textup), where\n$N=\\sum_\\mu|\\langle\\chi,\\la_0\\rangle|\\dim\\bN(\\chi)/2$. Here\n$\\la_0$ is the generator of the coweight lattice, which is\ndominant.\n\n\\textup{(2)} Suppose $G=\\PGL(2)$. Then $\\mathcal M_C(G,\\bN)$ is\nalso $\\xi^2 = \\delta\\eta^2 - \\delta^{N-1}$ where $N = \\sum_\\mu\n    |\\langle\\chi,\\la_0\\rangle|\\dim \\bN(\\chi)/2+1$. Furthermore we have\n$\\cR_{\\le\\la_0} = \\cR_{\\la_0}$ in this case, and generators are given by\n$\\eta = [\\cR_{\\la_0}]$, $\\xi = t[\\cR_{\\la_0}]$, $\\delta = t^2 \\in\n    H^4_{\\CC^\\times}(\\mathrm{pt})^{\\{\\pm 1\\}}$, up to multiplicative\nconstants, where $H^*_{\\CC^\\times}(\\mathrm{pt}) = \\CC[t]$. Here\n$\\la_0$ is again the generator of the coweight lattice, which is\ndominant. \\textup(It is the half of $\\la_0$ in \\textup{(1)}.\\textup).","source_id":"arXiv:1601.03586","cluster":"representation_theory","statement_sha256":"17a44166ec533a9f94b7b1beedf5ba91fa452a4de77feb8baf80988c1c3c3589","kind":"lemma","label":"Lemma 2","ref_key":"lem:SL2","name":"","order":87,"deps":[],"cite_keys":[],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03586","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"b2e79974e6e250f264382e70c5d3296d4abfc8d5c86da0ff1e8d9db5aafed33f","line":7791,"origin":"latex_env","retrieved_at":"2026-09-26T15:38:02+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v2","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_order_with_label_heuristic","detail":{"stripped_proof":false,"chars":965,"math_hints":145}}},"paper":{"source_id":"arXiv:1601.03586","title":"Towards a mathematical definition of Coulomb branches of $3$-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ gauge theories, II","abstract":"Consider the $3$-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ supersymmetric gauge theory associated with a compact Lie group $G_c$ and its quaternionic representation $\\mathbf M$. Physicists study its Coulomb branch, which is a noncompact hyper-Kähler manifold with an $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$-action, possibly with singularities. We give a mathematical definition of the Coulomb branch as an affine algebraic variety with $\\mathbb C^\\times$-action when $\\mathbf M$ is of a form $\\mathbf N\\oplus\\mathbf N^*$, as the second step of the proposal given in arXiv:1503.03676.","authors":["Alexander Braverman","Michael Finkelberg","Hiraku Nakajima"],"year":"2016","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03586","cluster":"representation_theory","macros":{"cal":"\\mathcal","Bbb":"\\mathbb","frak":"\\mathfrak","labelenumi":"\\textup{(\\theenumi)}","mathcal":"\\cal","mathbb":"\\Bbb","mathfrak":"\\frak","theenumi":"\\alph{enumi}","thmref":"\\ref{#1}","secref":"\\ref{#1}","lemref":"\\ref{#1}","propref":"\\ref{#1}","corref":"\\ref{#1}","subsecref":"\\ref{#1}","remref":"\\ref{#1}","defeq":"\\overset{\\operatorname{\\scriptstyle def.}}{=}","CC":"{\\mathbb C}","ZZ":"{\\mathbb Z}","QQ":"{\\mathbb Q}","RR":"{\\mathbb R}","proj":"{\\mathbb P}","CP":"\\proj","SL":"\\operatorname{\\rm SL}","SU":"\\operatorname{\\rm SU}","GL":"\\operatorname{GL}","PGL":"\\operatorname{PGL}","U":"\\operatorname{\\rm U}","grpSp":"\\operatorname{\\rm Sp}","algsl":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{sl}}","su":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{su}}","gl":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{gl}}","g":"{\\mathfrak g}","Spec":"\\operatorname{Spec}\\nolimits","Proj":"\\operatorname{Proj}\\nolimits","End":"\\operatorname{End}","Hom":"\\operatorname{Hom}","Ker":"\\operatorname{Ker}","Coker":"\\operatorname{Coker}","Ima":"\\operatorname{Im}","dim":"\\mathop{\\text{\\rm dim}}\\nolimits","codim":"\\mathop{\\text{\\rm codim}}\\nolimits","rank":"\\operatorname{rank}","tr":"\\operatorname{tr}","id":"\\operatorname{id}","HH":"\\mathbb H","dslash":"/\\!\\!/","bM":"\\mathbf M","bN":"